B. Outlining clear procedures for responding to acts of Sexual Misconduct
reported to the College;
C. Delivering education campaigns to students and employees to help them:
C.1. identify what behavior constitutes Sexual Misconduct;
C.2. understand how to report Sexual Misconduct;
C.3. recognize warning signs of potentially abusive behavior and ways to
reduce risks, and
C.4. learn about safe and positive options for bystander intervention to
prevent harm or intervene in situations where another person is at risk
of Sexual Misconduct.

D. Engaging in intake, investigative inquiry and resolution of complaints (through
the associated Sexual Misconduct procedures) that are prompt, fair, equitable
and independent of other investigations that may occur;
